  "raw_text": "Step 1: Recognize that \\(\\tan^{-1}(x)\\) and \\(\\cot^{-1}\\left(\\frac{1}{x}\\right)\\) are complementary angles. Specifically, for any \\(x \\neq 0\\), \\(\\tan^{-1}(x) + \\cot^{-1}\\left(\\frac{1}{x}\\right) = \\frac{\\pi}{2}\\).\n\nStep 2: Substitute this identity into the given equation:\n\\[\n\\sin\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{2}\\right) = \\frac{1}{3}.\n\\]\n\nStep 3: Simplify the left side of the equation using the known value of sine at \\(\\frac{\\pi}{2}\\):\n\\[\n1 = \\frac{1}{3}.\n\\]\n\nStep 4: This is a contradiction, indicating there are no values of \\(x\\) that satisfy the original equation under the condition \\(x \\neq 0\\).\n\nStep 5: Consider the case when \\(x = 0\\). However, \\(\\cot^{-1}\\left(\\frac{1}{0}\\right)\\) is undefined, so \\(x = 0\\) is not a valid solution.\n\nFinal answer: \\boxed{}<|im_end|>",
